  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nMid and upper-level synoptic pattern is fairly progressive for the \nnorthern third of the CONUS and into the Canadian Prairie Provinces. \nThis is currently resulting in modest mid-level ridging across the \neastern Plains and lower Missouri River Valley this afternoon, \ncontinuing the development of the surface anticyclone, but is \nstarting to push southeastward. This will continue to keep skies \nmostly clear this evening. We will need to monitor fog trends this \nevening and overnight, but if winds hold between 3 and 5 kts, its \ndevelopment may be inhibited. Low-lying areas sheltered from the \nwind and river valleys may still fog in this evening. Tuesday into \nWednesday morning, H5 short-wave and associated vort maxima travels \nout of the Rockies and de-amplifies the mid-level ridge, with dCVA \ndeveloping a surface cyclone in the northern High Plains. Ahead of \nthis lower-level flow turns more southwesterly, with increased WAA \nfrom the Central Plains toward the lower Missouri River Valley. This \nwill send temperatures into the mid 60s for most of the forecast \narea, with areas further south of Interstate 70 potentially hitting \nthe lower 70s. As the attendant cyclone continues to deepen, strong \ncold front will trail this system, and increase convergence heading \ninto Wednesday. \n\nThe rate of H5 height falls will continue to increase as the \nstronger vort maxima moves overhead, along with surface pressure \nfalls. While convergence will be increasing, current guidance \nfocuses the moisture transport from the Ozarks into eastern \nMissouri, while in central and western Missouri, the air remains \ndrier that may be the result of the dry conveyor belt wrapping \naround the low. Because of this, rain chances continue to remain \neast of Hwy. 63 Wednesday into Thursday, and mostly just outside of \nthe forecast area. Current ensemble probabilities maintain less than \n10 percent chance for measurable rain for our eastern zones, with \nhigher probabilities into eastern Missouri. There may be enough lift \nto generate some light sprinkle activity, but overall forcing and \nmoisture are aligning better further east.  \n\nThe trailing cold front is progged to move through late Wednesday \nNight and early Thursday morning, which will push temperatures back \ndown into the lower and mid 50s heading into the weekend. A strong \n500mb ridge is expected to amplify over the western CONUS during the \nweekend, reinforcing northwesterly flow into the Central CONUS which \nwill keep temperatures generally in the 50s. This may start \ndeamplify late Saturday, bringing mid-level height falls back across \nthe forecast area on Sunday, where light rain activity may be \npossible.",
